  <main>

    <section id="summary">
      <h2>1. Executive Summary</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Callisto is a mature, disciplined codebase &mdash; strict layering, <code>unsafe_code = "forbid"</code> everywhere, format-preserving CST editors instead of regex hacks, atomic-write-with-fsync persistence, and a CI gate (<code>just ci</code>) that actually enforces zero clippy warnings and full E2E coverage. Most of what this audit surfaces is exactly what you'd expect from a fast-moving session on top of a solid base: a handful of real correctness/security bugs in brand-new code (the publish pipeline hardening and the new <code>matrix.rs</code>/<code>config/pattern.rs</code> modules), a long tail of documentation drift, and dependency hygiene that has slipped slightly out of sync with the workspace's own stated policy. There is no evidence of systemic architectural rot &mdash; the layer boundaries hold, the AGPL/permissive split is intentional (if inconsistently labeled in docs), and the confirmed bugs are concentrated in exactly the areas the audit was told to focus on: <code>publish_client.rs</code>, the new <code>matrix.rs</code>/<code>pattern.rs</code> modules, and the CLI publish/status wiring.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <p style="margin-bottom:12px;"><strong>The five things worth acting on first:</strong></p>
        <ol class="top-actions">
          <li><strong>The version-inheritance parser bug is a self-inflicted outage.</strong> <code>cargo_publish</code>'s pre-publish version guard cannot parse <code>version.workspace = true</code> &mdash; the exact pattern all 10 of this repo's own crates use. Right now, running <code>callisto publish</code> on itself would hard-fail on every crate. Same-day fix and regression test.</li>
          <li><strong><code>PublishTarget::NuGet</code>/<code>GitHubRelease</code> are accepted but never wired to a publisher</strong>, and the release-tag gate doesn't check whether anything was actually published. A config typo can produce a git tag and a <code>ReleaseEntry</code> claiming a release happened when nothing was pushed to any registry &mdash; the highest-consequence bug in the batch, precisely because it fails silently.</li>
          <li><strong>Two real path-traversal / SSRF holes</strong> reachable from config or manifest files that get normal PR review but no automated containment check &mdash; an absolute or <code>..</code>-laden <code>changelog</code>/<code>changesets.dir</code> value can write or read outside the workspace root on CI, and an npm package's own <code>publishConfig.registry</code> field can redirect <code>npm publish</code>/<code>npm view</code> to an arbitrary host while <code>NPM_TOKEN</code> is in the environment. Both are cheap to fix with one shared path-containment helper and one registry allowlist check.</li>
          <li><strong>The <code>[[package-set]]</code> ecosystem-prefix feature is dead on arrival</strong> &mdash; and three separate audit passes independently rediscovered the same bug from three different angles, a strong signal it's real.</li>
          <li><strong>Documentation drift is real but not urgent</strong>: <code>callisto matrix</code> (a ~1,000-line flagship feature shipped this session) is entirely absent from <code>docs/00-design.md</code> and nearly absent from <code>docs/01-spec.md</code>; <code>callisto-vcs</code> has no crate-letter section at all.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <p><strong>Calibration:</strong> nothing here should be read as "this codebase is in trouble." The confirmed-critical findings are narrow, mechanical, and fast to fix (parser edge case, missing exhaustiveness check, missing path containment). The bulk of the 41 unverified findings are legitimate but low-stakes &mdash; undocumented traits, redundant git discoveries that cost milliseconds, unused dependencies. Treat the confirmed bucket as a punch list for this week; treat the unverified bucket as a backlog to burn down opportunistically, prioritizing the ones that touch the freshly-written publish/matrix/pattern surface over the long-stable Layer 1/2 crates.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="findings">
      <h2>2. Findings by Theme</h2>

      <h3 class="theme-title" id="t-architecture">Architecture &amp; Layering</h3>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-head">
          <span class="pill high">High</span>
          <span class="finding-title"><code>[[package-set]]</code> ecosystem-prefix matching is structurally dead &mdash; independently found three times</span>
        </div>
        <p class="locpath"><code>crates/callisto-graph/src/config/pattern.rs:1-45</code> (consumed at <code>resolve.rs:418-424</code>)</p>
        <div class="body">
          <p><span class="label">What</span><code>PackagePattern</code>'s doc comment promises <code>"cargo:pkg-*"</code> matches only Cargo packages, but <code>matches()</code> glob-matches only against <code>PackageId::name()</code>, which always has ecosystem prefixes stripped before it reaches the matcher. A glob compiled from <code>"cargo:pkg-*"</code> requires the target string to literally start with <code>cargo:</code>, which <code>name()</code> never returns &mdash; so any ecosystem-scoped rule matches zero packages, silently. <code>GraphError::PackageSetMatchedNothing</code> exists specifically to catch this but is constructed nowhere in the codebase &mdash; the safety net is itself dead code. No test in the workspace exercises an ecosystem-prefixed pattern.</p>
          <p><span class="label">Failure scenario</span>An operator writes <code>match = "cargo:internal-*"</code> intending to suppress publishing for internal Cargo crates while leaving an npm package of the same name unaffected. The rule matches nothing; the crate publishes normally with no warning.</p>
          <p><span class="label">Fix</span>Split an optional <code>ecosystem:</code>/<code>ecosystem/</code> prefix off the pattern in <code>PackagePattern::parse</code> (reuse <code>Ecosystem::from_prefix</code>), store it, and check <code>id.ecosystem()</code> before glob-matching the remainder against <code>id.name()</code>. Wire up <code>GraphError::PackageSetMatchedNothing</code>. Add a regression test asserting <code>"cargo:pkg-*"</code> matches a Cargo package and rejects an npm one of the same name.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-head">
          <span class="pill med">Medium</span>
          <span class="finding-title"><code>IdentityResolver</code> duplicates <code>callisto-manifests</code>' <code>package_name()</code> instead of reusing it</span>
          <span class="unverified-tag">not independently verified</span>
        </div>
        <p class="locpath"><code>crates/callisto-graph/src/identity.rs:12-107</code></p>
        <div class="body">
          <p><span class="label">What</span><code>IdentityResolver::resolve</code> hand-parses <code>Cargo.toml</code>/<code>package.json</code>/<code>pyproject.toml</code> directly with <code>toml_edit</code>/<code>serde_json</code> to extract package names, duplicating logic Layer 2's <code>callisto-manifests</code> already owns via <code>CargoManifest::package_name()</code>, <code>NpmManifest::package_name()</code>, <code>PythonManifest::package_name()</code>. This is exactly the drift the layer split exists to prevent &mdash; a future fix to PEP 621 dynamic-name handling won't propagate here, and the graph and manifest layers can disagree about a package's own identity.</p>
          <p><span class="label">Fix</span>Route <code>IdentityResolver::resolve</code> through <code>callisto-manifests</code>' existing <code>open_cached</code>/<code>Manifest</code> machinery and call <code>.package_name()</code> instead of re-parsing.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-head">
          <span class="pill med">Medium</span>
          <span class="finding-title"><code>CliCommandRunner</code> unconditionally streams subprocess stderr live, including from internal probes</span>
          <span class="unverified-tag">not independently verified</span>
        </div>
        <p class="locpath"><code>crates/callisto-cli/src/runner.rs:25-27, 84-97</code></p>
        <div class="body">
          <p><span class="label">What</span><code>run</code>/<code>run_with_timeout</code> echo raw subprocess stderr to the terminal unconditionally, with no "this is an internal probe" signal available to callers. <code>SubprocessRegistryClient::npm_is_published</code> runs <code>npm view</code> purely as an existence check where a 404-shaped failure is the expected "not yet published" signal &mdash; but its stderr streams live anyway, so every first-time npm publish shows what looks like a failure interleaved with a successful run.</p>
          <p><span class="label">Fix</span>Add a "probe, not action" mode to <code>CommandRunner</code> (or a <code>run_quiet</code> variant) so <code>is_published</code>-style calls don't stream live stderr; reserve live streaming for the actual mutating <code>publish()</code> call.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-head">
          <span class="pill low">Low</span>
          <span class="finding-title">Layer 1 crates (<code>callisto-model</code>, <code>callisto-format</code>) dev-depend on the AGPL-licensed <code>callisto-fixtures</code></span>
          <span class="unverified-tag">not independently verified</span>
        </div>
        <p class="locpath"><code>crates/callisto-model/Cargo.toml</code>, <code>crates/callisto-format/Cargo.toml</code></p>
        <div class="body">
          <p><span class="label">What</span>CLAUDE.md's invariant #4 states Layer 1 must "never depend on AGPL crates" with no dev-dependency carve-out; both crates dev-depend on AGPL-licensed <code>callisto-fixtures</code>. Doesn't affect published artifacts, but undermines the stated design goal.</p>
          <p><span class="label">Fix</span>Either document the dev-only exception explicitly in ARCHITECTURE.md, or extract shared test doubles into a small MIT/Apache dev-fixtures crate.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="theme-title" id="t-modeling">Domain Modeling &amp; Seams</h3>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-head">
          <span class="pill crit">Critical</span>
          <span class="finding-title"><code>PublishTarget::NuGet</code>/<code>GitHubRelease</code> pass validation and trigger release tagging with zero cross-validation, silently no-opping the real publish</span>
        </div>
        <p class="locpath"><code>crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/publish.rs:145-174, 216-230, 304-319</code>; <code>config/resolve.rs:148-164</code>; <code>walk.rs:191-193</code></p>
        <div class="body">
          <p><span class="label">What</span><code>PublishTarget</code> is a closed 6-variant enum but only 3 variants (CratesIo/Npm/Pypi) are wired into the publish pipeline via ad-hoc <code>.any(matches!(...))</code> checks &mdash; there is no exhaustive match forcing the compiler to catch an unhandled variant. <code>parse_publish_target</code> accepts <code>"nuget"</code>/<code>"github-release"</code> for <em>any</em> package regardless of its actual ecosystem, and <code>walk.rs</code> wholesale-replaces the manifest-inferred <code>publish_to</code> with no compatibility check. The "is this a release" gate only checks <code>publish_to</code> is non-empty and not all <code>None</code> &mdash; completely decoupled from whether anything was actually handed to a registry client.</p>
          <p><span class="label">Failure scenario</span><code>publish-to = ["nuget"]</code> on an ordinary Cargo crate (or a <code>crates-io</code> typo) causes <code>walk.rs</code> to overwrite the detected <code>CratesIo</code> target. On publish, <code>publishes_cargo/npm/pypi</code> are all false &mdash; nothing is uploaded anywhere &mdash; yet the release-tag block still fires, so a git tag and <code>ReleaseEntry</code> are created claiming a release happened. No diagnostic.</p>
          <p><span class="label">Fix</span>Replace the ad-hoc boolean checks with a single exhaustive <code>match target { ... }</code> per package/target. Validate at config-load time that a configured target's <code>.ecosystem()</code> matches the package's real ecosystem, rejecting mismatches as a config error. Gate release-tag creation on "a publish actually succeeded/was attempted" rather than "target != None".</p>
          <p class="verified-note">&#10003; Independently confirmed: zero grep hits for "NuGet" dispatch in publish.rs/publish_client.rs; release gate checks only non-empty/non-None.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-head">
          <span class="pill med">Medium</span>
          <span class="finding-title"><code>detect_npm_role</code> always sets <code>abi: None</code>, but <code>role_to_triple</code>'s Linux entries all require <code>Some("gnu")</code>/<code>Some("musl")</code></span>
          <span class="unverified-tag">not independently verified</span>
        </div>
        <p class="locpath"><code>crates/callisto-graph/src/walk.rs:355-383</code></p>
        <div class="body">
          <p><span class="label">What</span>npm's <code>os</code>/<code>cpu</code> fields carry no ABI info, so disk-discovered roles always have <code>abi: None</code>, but <code>napi.rs::role_to_triple</code>'s match table has no <code>(linux, &lt;arch&gt;, None)</code> arm. <code>ManifestRole::Platform</code> is populated by two independent producers with no shared invariant between them &mdash; real disk-discovered Linux napi packages can never round-trip.</p>
          <p><span class="label">Fix</span>Give <code>detect_npm_role</code> access to the same triple-recognition table <code>napi.rs</code> uses, or make <code>role_to_triple</code> treat <code>abi: None</code> as a wildcard.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-head">
          <span class="pill med">Medium</span>
          <span class="finding-title"><code>PreMajorInferencePolicy</code> models a closed 3-state policy as two independent public bools, permitting a 4th, undefined combination</span>
          <span class="unverified-tag">not independently verified</span>
        </div>
        <p class="locpath"><code>crates/callisto-graph/src/config/resolve.rs:135-146, 204-219</code></p>
        <div class="body">
          <p><span class="label">What</span>Only 3 combinations are ever produced by <code>parse_pre_major_policy</code>, but the struct's two <code>pub bool</code> fields let any caller construct the unparsed, untested 4th state (<code>feat_to_patch: true, breaking_to_minor: false</code>) via a plain struct literal.</p>
          <p><span class="label">Fix</span>Replace with <code>enum PreMajorInferencePolicy { Off, Conservative, ConservativeFeat }</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-head">
          <span class="pill med">Medium</span>
          <span class="finding-title"><code>PackageId::matches()</code> returns a bare <code>bool</code> for an operation its own doc comment describes as ambiguity-prone &mdash; has already caused one real bug</span>
          <span class="unverified-tag">not independently verified</span>
        </div>
        <p class="locpath"><code>crates/callisto-model/src/identity.rs:103-154</code></p>
        <div class="body">
          <p><span class="label">What</span>The doc comment documents that <code>Bare("foo")</code> matches every <code>Prefixed(_, "foo")</code> and that callers "must" collect-and-error on 2+ matches, but nothing enforces this. <code>status.rs</code>'s own regression test comment confirms this exact misuse already shipped as a real bug that silently attached a changeset to two packages. 5+ non-test call sites exist today with no compiler-enforced safe alternative.</p>
          <p><span class="label">Fix</span>Add a safe <code>resolve_unique()</code> API and steer callers toward it; consider <code>#[deprecated]</code>-hinting raw <code>matches()</code> for resolution use.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="finding">
        <div class="finding-head">
          <span class="pill low">Low</span>
          <span class="finding-title"><code>PackageIdParseError::PathTraversal</code> is reused for the unrelated leading-hyphen check, producing a factually false error message</span>
          <span class="unverified-tag">not independently verified</span>
        </div>
        <p class="locpath"><code>crates/callisto-model/src/identity.rs:31-32, 46-47, 64-65, 224-227</code></p>
        <div class="body">
          <p><span class="label">What</span><code>PackageId::parse("-x")</code> returns an error whose hardcoded message claims the string "contains path traversal <code>..</code>" even though it contains no <code>..</code>.</p>
